```

Wait, there's a problem with `StreamController` - it needs to be closed. But in this case we're returning the stream and the consumer will dispose of it... Actually no, StreamBuilder doesn't dispose the stream itself. The controller should ideally be closed when the subscription is cancelled.

Let me think about this differently. A better approach would use `Stream.fromFutures` or a `BehaviorSubject`, but those require additional dependencies.

Actually, looking at the problem more carefully:
1. We want to emit cached data immediately (if any)
2. Then we want to emit fresh data when it arrives from network
3. The stream should ideally be closed when no longer needed

A cleaner approach using `StreamController` with `onCancel`:

```dart
@override
Stream<List<NewsModel>> loadNewsFeedStream(NewsFeedFilter filter) {
  final controller = StreamController<List<NewsModel>>(
    onCancel: (controller) {
      controller.close();
    },
  );

  // First, emit cached data immediately
  _dbDatasource.getNewsList().then((cached) {
    if (!controller.isClosed && controller.hasListener) {
      controller.add(cached);
    }
  }).catchError((_) {
    if (!controller.isClosed && controller.hasListener) {
      controller.add([]);
    }
  });

  // Then fetch fresh data in background
  _restDatasource.getNewsList().then((fresh) async {
    await _dbDatasource.saveNewsList(fresh);
    if (!controller.isClosed && controller.hasListener) {
      controller.add(fresh);
    }
  }).catchError((_) {
    // On error, keep showing cached data - do nothing
  });

  return controller.stream;
